From: Pedro Baltazar <pedrobtz@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH P1] ggml : accumulate the MEAN backward gradient with repeat

Upstream pull request: (not opened yet; see design §9.3)

ggml_mean() reduces each row to one value, so its gradient is the
incoming gradient divided by ne[0], repeated across the row. The backward
pass added it with ggml_add1_or_set(), which asserts ggml_is_scalar(b)
once a second consumer has already contributed a gradient: a LayerNorm
written with ggml_mean() aborted in ggml_opt_init at ggml.c:2158. Repeat
the scaled gradient to src0's shape and accumulate it with
ggml_add_or_set(), as SUM_ROWS does.

--- a/src/ggml.c
+++ b/src/ggml.c
@@ -6885,7 +6885,7 @@
         } break;
         case GGML_OP_MEAN: {
             if (src0_needs_grads) {
-                ggml_add1_or_set(ctx, cgraph, isrc0, ggml_scale_impl(ctx, grad, 1.0f/src0->ne[0], 0.0, false));
+                ggml_add_or_set(ctx, cgraph, isrc0, ggml_repeat(ctx, ggml_scale_impl(ctx, grad, 1.0f/src0->ne[0], 0.0, false), src0));
             }
         } break;
         case GGML_OP_REPEAT: {
